{%- comment -%}
  App Embed Block (target: body) - emits schema.org JSON-LD for the current
  page using NATIVE Liquid objects (product / collection / article / shop), so
  rich-snippet markup is produced automatically with no admin API call. Every
  dynamic value is passed through the `json` filter, which JSON-encodes the
  string AND escapes `<` (so a value containing "</script>" cannot break out
  of the surrounding <script> element). Absolute URLs are built from
  `shop.url` (canonical, no trailing slash) + the object's root-relative
  `.url`. Enable once - no placement needed.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- liquid
  assign want_org = block.settings.enable_organization
  assign want_product = block.settings.enable_product
  assign want_collection = block.settings.enable_collection
  assign want_article = block.settings.enable_article
  assign want_breadcrumb = block.settings.enable_breadcrumb
  assign want_faq = block.settings.enable_faq
  assign want_video = block.settings.enable_video

  assign logo_url = ''
  if block.settings.logo
    assign logo_url = block.settings.logo | image_url: width: 512
  elsif shop.brand.logo
    assign logo_url = shop.brand.logo | image_url: width: 512
  endif
  # `image_url` returns a PROTOCOL-RELATIVE URL ("//cdn.shopify.com/..."), and
  # Google requires an absolute one. Dawn prepends "https:" unconditionally; we
  # test the first two characters instead, so an already-absolute URL can never
  # become "https:https://...". Same treatment everywhere an image is emitted.
  if logo_url != ''
    assign logo_scheme = logo_url | slice: 0, 2
    if logo_scheme == '//'
      assign logo_url = logo_url | prepend: 'https:'
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- if want_org -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="organization">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Organization",
  "name": {{ shop.name | json }},
  "url": {{ shop.url | json }}
  {%- if logo_url != '' -%}, "logo": {{ logo_url | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- assign social_lines = block.settings.social_urls | newline_to_br | split: '<br />' -%}
  {%- assign social_clean = '' -%}
  {%- for s in social_lines -%}
    {%- assign st = s | strip -%}
    {%- if st != '' -%}
      {%- if social_clean == '' -%}{%- assign social_clean = st -%}{%- else -%}{%- assign social_clean = social_clean | append: '|' | append: st -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}
  {%- if social_clean != '' -%}
    , "sameAs": [
    {%- assign parts = social_clean | split: '|' -%}
    {%- for p in parts -%}{{ p | json }}{%- unless forloop.last -%}, {%- endunless -%}{%- endfor -%}
    ]
  {%- endif -%}
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Product. Built to stand ALONE: the theme's own `{{ product | structured_data }}`
  is expected to be removed later, so this entity has to carry everything Google
  needs by itself.

  Two invariants worth stating, because both are easy to break:

  1. The output does NOT depend on `?variant=`. Shopify strips the variant
     parameter from the canonical URL, so a page that emitted a different
     entity per variant URL would hand Google several identities for one page.
     `@id` and `url` are therefore always the canonical product URL, and the
     selected variant's price lives in its own Offer inside the array. That
     also means `selected_or_first_available_variant` must not be read here.
  2. `@id` uses Dawn's exact scheme (`/products/<handle>#product`,
     `...?variant=<id>#offer`) so removing the theme's schema later does not
     change the identity of anything.
  3. Nothing in this entity may be INVENTED. Every value has to trace back to
     a native field or a merchant-set metafield — Shopify App Store
     requirement 1.1.4 ("use only factual information") applies to structured
     data just as it does to visible copy, and markup is read by Google and by
     shoppers alike.

     `priceValidUntil` is the property that broke this rule: it was
     synthesized as "now + 1 year" on every Offer, i.e. this app told the
     world that every price in the shop is guaranteed for exactly a year.
     No store datum says that. It is now read from the merchant's
     `custom.price_valid_until` product metafield (type `date`/`date_time`)
     and omitted otherwise — including when the date has already passed,
     because a stale guarantee is as untrue as an invented one. Omitting it
     is cheap: Google lists the property as RECOMMENDED, so its absence is a
     non-critical Search Console warning and costs no rich result.
     app/services/structured-data.service.ts follows the identical rule.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if want_product and request.page_type == 'product' and product -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign p_url = shop.url | append: product.url
    # Relative @id, exactly as Dawn emits it — JSON-LD resolves it against the
    # page URL, and matching Dawn keeps the identity stable across the switch.
    assign p_id = product.url | append: '#product'
    # `page_description` is Shopify's own meta description for the CURRENT
    # page and follows the active locale; the raw `global.description_tag`
    # metafield does not, so a Spanish page shipped a German description while
    # its `name` was correctly Spanish. `product.description` is a translated
    # native field and makes a safe fallback.
    # The SEO meta description is stored HTML-ENCODED, and `strip_html` only
    # removes tags — it does not decode entities, so `&quot;` and `&amp;` went
    # into the JSON verbatim while the native `name` (never encoded) looked
    # fine. Decode before `| json`, `&amp;` LAST: doing it first would turn the
    # literal text "&amp;quot;" into a quote character.
    assign p_desc = page_description | default: product.description | strip_html | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&#34;', '"' | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&apos;', "'" | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&nbsp;', ' ' | replace: '&amp;', '&' | truncate: 500
    assign p_currency = cart.currency.iso_code | default: shop.currency
    # Shopify "rating" metafield value is a rating object: .rating / .scale_max
    assign r_obj = product.metafields.reviews.rating.value
    assign r_count = product.metafields.reviews.rating_count.value
    assign p_mpn = product.metafields.custom.mpn.value
    # priceValidUntil: merchant metafield only, never synthesized, expired
    # dates dropped — invariant 3 above has the compliance reasoning.
    #
    # Both sides of the comparison run through the SAME `%Y-%m-%d | %s` chain,
    # and that symmetry is the whole point of the detour:
    #  - `| date: '%Y-%m-%d'` first reduces both to a calendar DAY, so a
    #    date_time metafield stays valid for the rest of its last day, and a
    #    date metafield (UTC midnight) is not compared against a shop-local
    #    midnight — that mismatch dropped the final valid day west of UTC.
    #  - `| plus: 0` then forces INTEGER comparison. Liquid would otherwise
    #    compare the two as strings: `>=` on strings is lexicographic, and an
    #    unparseable metafield ("soon") survives `date` unchanged and would
    #    sort ABOVE any ISO date — publishing "soon" as priceValidUntil, i.e.
    #    the very fabrication this code exists to prevent. As an integer it
    #    coerces to 0 and is dropped.
    assign p_valid_until = ''
    assign pvu_raw = product.metafields.custom.price_valid_until.value
    if pvu_raw != blank
      assign pvu_ts = pvu_raw | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
      assign pvu_today_ts = 'now' | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | date: '%s' | plus: 0
      if pvu_ts >= pvu_today_ts
        assign p_valid_until = pvu_raw | date: '%Y-%m-%d'
      endif
    endif
    assign variant_count = product.variants | size
    assign max_variant_offers = 25

    # Above the threshold there are no Offers to carry sku/gtin, and Google
    # Merchant listings need SOME product identifier — so the AggregateOffer
    # case (like the single-variant case) puts them on the Product. `first`,
    # never `selected_or_first_available_variant`: the output must not depend
    # on `?variant=`.
    assign id_variant = nil
    if variant_count == 1 or variant_count > max_variant_offers
      assign id_variant = product.variants | first
    endif
    assign id_gtin = ''
    assign id_gtin_key = ''
    if id_variant
      assign id_gtin = id_variant.barcode | default: '' | remove: '-' | remove: ' '
      assign id_gtin_len = id_gtin | size
      if id_gtin_len == 8
        assign id_gtin_key = 'gtin8'
      elsif id_gtin_len == 12
        assign id_gtin_key = 'gtin12'
      elsif id_gtin_len == 13
        assign id_gtin_key = 'gtin13'
      elsif id_gtin_len == 14
        assign id_gtin_key = 'gtin14'
      elsif id_gtin != ''
        assign id_gtin_key = 'gtin'
      endif
    endif

    # AggregateOffer inputs. Availability has to be aggregated rather than
    # omitted: a product whose 30 variants are ALL sold out would otherwise
    # hand Google prices with no availability signal at all — the same false
    # claim the per-variant branch exists to avoid. lowPrice/highPrice come
    # from the AVAILABLE variants while there are any, so the advertised price
    # is one a shopper can actually pay.
    assign agg_avail_count = 0
    assign agg_backorder_count = 0
    assign agg_has_low = false
    assign agg_low = 0
    assign agg_high = 0
    for v in product.variants
      if v.available
        assign agg_avail_count = agg_avail_count | plus: 1
        if v.inventory_management != blank and v.inventory_quantity <= 0 and v.inventory_policy == 'continue'
          assign agg_backorder_count = agg_backorder_count | plus: 1
        endif
        if agg_has_low == false or v.price < agg_low
          assign agg_low = v.price
          assign agg_has_low = true
        endif
        if v.price > agg_high
          assign agg_high = v.price
        endif
      endif
    endfor
    if agg_avail_count == 0
      assign agg_low = product.price_min
      assign agg_high = product.price_max
      assign agg_availability = 'https://schema.org/OutOfStock'
    elsif agg_backorder_count == agg_avail_count
      # Every purchasable variant is a backorder — saying InStock here is the
      # "ships in three weeks" lie in aggregate form.
      assign agg_availability = 'https://schema.org/BackOrder'
    else
      assign agg_availability = 'https://schema.org/InStock'
    endif
  -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    Images as an array (Google prefers several, and 1:1/4:3/16:9 crops of the
    same shot are explicitly recommended). Capped so a product with 200 media
    items can't blow up the page.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture p_images -%}
    {%- assign img_printed = 0 -%}
    {%- for p_image in product.images limit: 10 -%}
      {%- assign img_url = p_image | image_url: width: 1200 -%}
      {%- assign img_scheme = img_url | slice: 0, 2 -%}
      {%- if img_scheme == '//' -%}{%- assign img_url = img_url | prepend: 'https:' -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- if img_printed > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}{{ img_url | json }}
      {%- assign img_printed = img_printed | plus: 1 -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    One Offer per variant — NOT AggregateOffer. Google derives the price range
    from an offer array by itself and additionally gets per-variant SKU, GTIN
    and availability, all of which AggregateOffer throws away. Past the
    threshold the markup would outweigh the page, so a shop with very many
    variants falls back to AggregateOffer for size alone.

    `availability` is computed per variant rather than from `product.available`:
    once the theme's schema is gone this is the only statement Google sees, and
    "InStock" for something that ships in three weeks is simply false.
    `inventory_quantity` / `inventory_policy` are used for the decision only and
    never written into the markup.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- capture p_offers -%}
    {%- if variant_count > max_variant_offers -%}
      {
        "@type": "AggregateOffer",
        "priceCurrency": {{ p_currency | json }},
        "lowPrice": {{ agg_low | divided_by: 100.0 | round: 2 | append: '' | json }},
        "highPrice": {{ agg_high | divided_by: 100.0 | round: 2 | append: '' | json }},
        "offerCount": {{ variant_count }},
        "availability": {{ agg_availability | json }},
        "itemCondition": "https://schema.org/NewCondition",
        "url": {{ p_url | json }}
        {%- if p_valid_until != '' -%}, "priceValidUntil": {{ p_valid_until | json }}{%- endif -%}
      }
    {%- else -%}
    [
      {%- assign off_printed = 0 -%}
      {%- for v in product.variants -%}
        {%- liquid
          assign v_url = p_url | append: '?variant=' | append: v.id
          assign v_id = product.url | append: '?variant=' | append: v.id | append: '#offer'

          if v.inventory_management == blank
            assign v_avail = 'https://schema.org/InStock'
          elsif v.available == false
            assign v_avail = 'https://schema.org/OutOfStock'
          elsif v.inventory_quantity <= 0 and v.inventory_policy == 'continue'
            assign v_avail = 'https://schema.org/BackOrder'
          else
            assign v_avail = 'https://schema.org/InStock'
          endif

          assign v_gtin = v.barcode | default: '' | remove: '-' | remove: ' '
          assign v_gtin_len = v_gtin | size
          assign v_gtin_key = ''
          if v_gtin_len == 8
            assign v_gtin_key = 'gtin8'
          elsif v_gtin_len == 12
            assign v_gtin_key = 'gtin12'
          elsif v_gtin_len == 13
            assign v_gtin_key = 'gtin13'
          elsif v_gtin_len == 14
            assign v_gtin_key = 'gtin14'
          elsif v_gtin != ''
            assign v_gtin_key = 'gtin'
          endif
        -%}
        {%- if off_printed > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
        {
          "@type": "Offer",
          "@id": {{ v_id | json }},
          "price": {{ v.price | divided_by: 100.0 | round: 2 | append: '' | json }},
          "priceCurrency": {{ p_currency | json }},
          "availability": {{ v_avail | json }},
          "itemCondition": "https://schema.org/NewCondition",
          "url": {{ v_url | json }}
          {%- if p_valid_until != '' -%}, "priceValidUntil": {{ p_valid_until | json }}{%- endif -%}
          {%- if v.sku != blank -%}, "sku": {{ v.sku | json }}{%- endif -%}
          {%- if v_gtin_key != '' -%}, "{{ v_gtin_key }}": {{ v_gtin | json }}{%- endif -%}
        }
        {%- assign off_printed = off_printed | plus: 1 -%}
      {%- endfor -%}
    ]
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="product">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "Product",
  "@id": {{ p_id | json }},
  "name": {{ product.title | json }},
  "url": {{ p_url | json }}
  {%- if p_desc != '' -%}, "description": {{ p_desc | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if img_printed > 0 -%}, "image": [{{ p_images | strip }}]{%- endif -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    SKU and GTIN sit on the OFFERS, not here: with several variants a
    product-level value would have to pick one, and picking the selected
    variant is exactly the `?variant=`-dependent output invariant 1 rules out.
    A single-variant product keeps them at product level too, where they are
    unambiguous and Merchant Center reads them most reliably.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if id_variant -%}
    {%- if id_variant.sku != blank -%}, "sku": {{ id_variant.sku | json }}{%- endif -%}
    {%- if id_gtin_key != '' -%}, "{{ id_gtin_key }}": {{ id_gtin | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- if p_mpn != blank -%}, "mpn": {{ p_mpn | json }}{%- endif -%}
  , "brand": { "@type": "Brand", "name": {{ product.vendor | default: shop.name | json }} }
  , "offers": {{ p_offers | strip }}
  {%- if r_obj and r_obj.rating and r_count and r_count > 0 -%}
  , "aggregateRating": {
      "@type": "AggregateRating",
      "ratingValue": {{ r_obj.rating | plus: 0 }},
      "bestRating": {{ r_obj.scale_max | default: 5 | plus: 0 }},
      "reviewCount": {{ r_count | plus: 0 }}
    }
  {%- endif -%}
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  FAQ (plan §C2): opt-in (`enable_faq`, default false), product pages only.
  Sourced from the product metafield `custom.faq` (type `json`, a list of
  `{question, answer}`) via `METAFIELDS_SET` — no admin API call needed here,
  it's read straight off the native `product` object. Built into a `capture`
  first so we only emit the <script> block when at least one entry has both a
  non-blank question and answer (an empty `mainEntity` is invalid FAQPage
  markup); the comma between entries is placed via a counter (not join/split)
  since question/answer text can contain any character. Every value goes
  through `| json` per this block's escaping convention.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if want_faq and request.page_type == 'product' and product -%}
  {%- assign faq_items = product.metafields.custom.faq.value -%}
  {%- capture faq_body -%}
    {%- assign faq_printed = 0 -%}
    {%- for faq_item in faq_items -%}
      {%- if faq_item.question != blank and faq_item.answer != blank -%}
        {%- if faq_printed > 0 -%},{%- endif -%}
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": {{ faq_item.question | json }},
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": {{ faq_item.answer | json }}
          }
        }
        {%- assign faq_printed = faq_printed | plus: 1 -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endcapture -%}
  {%- if faq_body != blank -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="faq">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {{ faq_body }}
  ]
}
</script>
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if want_collection and request.page_type == 'collection' and collection -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign c_url = shop.url | append: collection.url
    # Locale-aware, same reasoning as the product description above.
    assign c_desc = page_description | default: collection.description | strip_html | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&#34;', '"' | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&apos;', "'" | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&nbsp;', ' ' | replace: '&amp;', '&' | truncate: 500
  -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="collection">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "CollectionPage",
  "name": {{ collection.title | json }},
  "url": {{ c_url | json }}
  {%- if c_desc != '' -%}, "description": {{ c_desc | json }}{%- endif -%}
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- if want_article and request.page_type == 'article' and article -%}
  {%- liquid
    assign a_url = shop.url | append: article.url
    assign a_desc = page_description | default: article.excerpt_or_content | strip_html | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&#34;', '"' | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&apos;', "'" | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&nbsp;', ' ' | replace: '&amp;', '&' | truncate: 500
    assign a_img = ''
    if article.image
      assign a_img = article.image | image_url: width: 1200
      assign a_img_scheme = a_img | slice: 0, 2
      if a_img_scheme == '//'
        assign a_img = a_img | prepend: 'https:'
      endif
    endif
  -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="article">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": {{ article.title | json }},
  "url": {{ a_url | json }},
  "mainEntityOfPage": {{ a_url | json }},
  "datePublished": {{ article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z' | json }},
  "dateModified": {{ article.updated_at | default: article.published_at | date: '%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%S%z' | json }},
  "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": {{ article.author | default: shop.name | json }} },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": {{ shop.name | json }}
    {%- if logo_url != '' -%}, "logo": { "@type": "ImageObject", "url": {{ logo_url | json }} }{%- endif -%}
  }
  {%- if a_desc != '' -%}, "description": {{ a_desc | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if a_img != '' -%}, "image": {{ a_img | json }}{%- endif -%}
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  VideoObject: product pages, from the native `product.media`. Covers Shopify-
  hosted videos AND external ones (YouTube/Vimeo), because a merchant who
  embedded a YouTube video did the same job and expects the same treatment.

  Emitted as one <script> PER video rather than one array: each object stays
  self-contained with its own @context, which is this block's convention for
  every other type, and a video whose data is incomplete simply doesn't print
  instead of poisoning a shared array.

  Two properties decide whether it is emitted at all: a thumbnail and a URL to
  the video (contentUrl for hosted, embedUrl for external). Without either
  there is nothing a consumer could do with the entry.

  `uploadDate` is one of Google's REQUIRED properties for the video rich
  result and Liquid's media objects carry no creation date. It comes from two
  metafields instead, merchant first: `custom.video_upload_date` (a date the
  merchant set for the product) wins, otherwise the per-media map in
  `custom.video_upload_dates`, which the app maintains from the Admin API's
  `File.createdAt` during the product sync. Both absent ⇒ the property is
  OMITTED, never guessed — same rule as `custom.price_valid_until` above.

  `duration` is milliseconds in Liquid and ISO-8601 in schema.org, hence the
  divide/modulo.

  The cap counts VIDEOS EMITTED, not media scanned: `product.media` mixes
  images and videos, so a `limit:` on the loop would silently skip the video of
  a product whose first ten media are photos. External videos have no duration and skip the property.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if want_video and request.page_type == 'product' and product -%}
  {%- assign v_desc = product.description | strip_html | replace: '&quot;', '"' | replace: '&#34;', '"' | replace: '&#39;', "'" | replace: '&apos;', "'" | replace: '&lt;', '<' | replace: '&gt;', '>' | replace: '&nbsp;', ' ' | replace: '&amp;', '&' | truncate: 500 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Two date sources, merchant first. `custom.video_upload_dates` is the map
    the APP maintains from the Admin API's `File.createdAt` (one entry per
    media id, written by the product sync — see services/seo/video-schema.*),
    which is what makes the rich result reachable without anyone typing a date.
    `custom.video_upload_date` stays as the merchant's manual override for the
    whole product: they may know the real first-publication date (a video
    that ran on YouTube long before it was added here), and a person's stated
    fact outranks our derived one.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign v_upload_override = '' -%}
  {%- assign vud_raw = product.metafields.custom.video_upload_date.value -%}
  {%- if vud_raw != blank -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      The metafield is type `date` and carries no time, but Google rejects a
      date-only uploadDate ("invalid date/time value" + "missing timezone").
      Midday UTC is the one stamp that lands on the merchant's chosen day in
      every real timezone - midnight would read as the previous day west of
      Greenwich and so would misreport a date a person stated.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign v_upload_override = vud_raw | date: '%Y-%m-%d' | append: 'T12:00:00Z' -%}
  {%- endif -%}
  {%- assign v_upload_map = product.metafields.custom.video_upload_dates.value -%}
  {%- assign v_printed = 0 -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    Dedup set for the gallery pass below, keyed by "<host>|<id>" and NOT by the
    URL: the same YouTube video reaches this block as a watch link, a youtu.be
    short link or a native external_video, and three spellings of one video
    would print three VideoObjects - the exact double markup this section is
    otherwise about. Comma-joined with a leading comma so a `,needle,` check
    cannot match a partial id.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign v_seen_ids = ',' -%}
  {%- for v_media in product.media -%}
    {%- if v_printed >= 5 -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
    {%- if v_media.media_type == 'video' or v_media.media_type == 'external_video' -%}
      {%- liquid
        # Per-video date from the app's map, keyed by the NUMERIC media id
        # (Liquid's `media.id` is the number, the map is written with the same
        # form). `| append: ''` forces the string key a JSON object has.
        assign v_upload = v_upload_override
        if v_upload == '' and v_upload_map
          assign v_media_key = v_media.id | append: ''
          assign v_upload = v_upload_map[v_media_key] | default: ''
        endif

        assign v_thumb = ''
        if v_media.preview_image
          assign v_thumb = v_media.preview_image | image_url: width: 1200
          assign v_scheme = v_thumb | slice: 0, 2
          if v_scheme == '//'
            assign v_thumb = v_thumb | prepend: 'https:'
          endif
        endif

        assign v_content = ''
        assign v_embed = ''
        if v_media.media_type == 'video'
          assign v_mp4 = v_media.sources | where: 'format', 'mp4' | last
          if v_mp4 == nil
            assign v_mp4 = v_media.sources | last
          endif
          assign v_content = v_mp4.url
          # Shopify source URLs are PROTOCOL-RELATIVE ("//cdn...") and Google
          # requires an absolute contentUrl - same treatment as the images.
          if v_content != blank
            assign v_content_scheme = v_content | slice: 0, 2
            if v_content_scheme == '//'
              assign v_content = v_content | prepend: 'https:'
            endif
          endif
        else
          if v_media.host == 'youtube'
            assign v_embed = 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/' | append: v_media.external_id
          elsif v_media.host == 'vimeo'
            assign v_embed = 'https://player.vimeo.com/video/' | append: v_media.external_id
          endif
        endif

        # ONE flag instead of a compound condition on the emission guard:
        # Liquid has no operator precedence and evaluates `and`/`or` right to
        # left, so `a and b or a and c` is a puzzle rather than a condition.
        assign v_has_url = false
        if v_content != blank or v_embed != blank
          assign v_has_url = true
        endif

        assign v_duration = ''
        if v_media.duration and v_media.duration > 0
          assign v_secs = v_media.duration | divided_by: 1000
          assign v_mins = v_secs | divided_by: 60
          assign v_rest = v_secs | modulo: 60
          capture v_duration
            echo 'PT'
            echo v_mins
            echo 'M'
            echo v_rest
            echo 'S'
          endcapture
        endif
      -%}
      {%- if v_thumb != blank and v_has_url -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="video">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "VideoObject",
  "name": {{ v_media.alt | default: product.title | json }},
  "thumbnailUrl": [{{ v_thumb | json }}]
  {%- if v_desc != blank -%}, "description": {{ v_desc | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if v_upload != blank -%}, "uploadDate": {{ v_upload | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if v_content != blank -%}, "contentUrl": {{ v_content | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if v_embed != blank -%}, "embedUrl": {{ v_embed | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if v_duration != blank -%}, "duration": {{ v_duration | json }}{%- endif -%}
}
</script>
        {%- assign v_printed = v_printed | plus: 1 -%}
        {%- if v_media.media_type == 'external_video' -%}
          {%- assign v_seen_ids = v_seen_ids | append: v_media.host | append: '|' | append: v_media.external_id | append: ',' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endfor -%}

  {%- comment -%}
    PLAN_MARKUP_ACTIVATION Phase 3 - videos that are NOT in product.media.

    Measured on a live shop (patis-universe.com, 2026-08): across all 41
    products not one had a native product.media video, while the YouTube video
    the shop actually shows sat in two VARIANT metafields that the variant
    gallery block reads. The VideoObject feature therefore had zero coverage
    there. The two sources, both per variant:

      custom.variant_gallery_order  - JSON [{kind, value}], the merchant's
                                      drag order; entries with kind == 'url'
      custom.variant_external_videos - list.url, appended after the files

    Both are collected into ONE url-encoded, comma-joined string first and
    emitted by ONE loop afterwards. Two emission bodies for two sources is how
    the URL parser in variant-gallery-embed.liquid came to exist three times
    and then drift apart; one accumulation pass is cheaper than that in every
    sense, budget included. url_encode is what makes the comma join safe - a
    Vimeo privacy hash or a ?list=a,b,c would otherwise split into fragments.

    Deduplicated PRODUCT-WIDE, because the same video typically hangs on every
    variant: one VideoObject per variant would be precisely the duplicate
    markup Phase 1 exists to prevent.

    uploadDate: a URL entry has no `File` record, so `File.createdAt` - and
    with it the whole custom.video_upload_dates map the product sync writes -
    does not apply here. That leaves the merchant's own
    custom.video_upload_date, and when that is unset the property is OMITTED,
    never estimated. A YouTube video does have a public publication date, but
    it is reachable only through the YouTube Data API (own provider, own key,
    own quota) and is an explicit non-goal. Invariant 3 above governs: nothing
    in this entity may be invented.

    Vimeo entries print nothing on purpose. thumbnailUrl is required for the
    rich result and Vimeo exposes no thumbnail URL derivable from the id;
    substituting the product image would answer "what does this video look
    like" with a picture of something else.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- assign v_gallery_urls = '' -%}
  {%- liquid
    for v_variant in product.variants
      assign v_order_mf = v_variant.metafields.custom.variant_gallery_order
      if v_order_mf != blank and v_order_mf.value != blank
        for v_entry in v_order_mf.value
          if v_entry.kind == 'url'
            assign v_tok = v_entry.value | append: '' | strip | url_encode
            if v_tok != blank
              assign v_gallery_urls = v_gallery_urls | append: v_tok | append: ','
            endif
          endif
        endfor
      endif
      assign v_extvid_mf = v_variant.metafields.custom.variant_external_videos
      if v_extvid_mf != blank and v_extvid_mf.value != blank
        for v_extvid in v_extvid_mf.value
          assign v_tok = v_extvid | append: '' | strip | url_encode
          if v_tok != blank
            assign v_gallery_urls = v_gallery_urls | append: v_tok | append: ','
          endif
        endfor
      endif
    endfor
  -%}
  {%- if v_gallery_urls != blank -%}
    {%- assign v_gallery_toks = v_gallery_urls | split: ',' -%}
    {%- for v_tok in v_gallery_toks -%}
      {%- if v_printed >= 5 -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- assign v_gurl = v_tok | url_decode -%}
      {%- comment -%} host+id via snippets/cp-external-video (render is scope-isolated, so the answer comes back as captured text; the strip is mandatory - capture keeps every whitespace byte) {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- capture v_pair -%}{%- render 'cp-external-video', url: v_gurl -%}{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- assign v_pair = v_pair | strip -%}
      {%- assign v_ghost = v_pair | split: '|' | first | default: '' -%}
      {%- assign v_gid = v_pair | split: '|' | last | default: '' -%}
      {%- if v_ghost == 'youtube' and v_gid != blank -%}
        {%- assign v_needle = ',' | append: v_pair | append: ',' -%}
        {%- unless v_seen_ids contains v_needle -%}
          {%- assign v_seen_ids = v_seen_ids | append: v_pair | append: ',' -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="video">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "VideoObject",
  "name": {{ product.title | json }},
  "thumbnailUrl": [{{ 'https://img.youtube.com/vi/' | append: v_gid | append: '/hqdefault.jpg' | json }}],
  "embedUrl": {{ 'https://www.youtube.com/embed/' | append: v_gid | json }}
  {%- if v_desc != blank -%}, "description": {{ v_desc | json }}{%- endif -%}
  {%- if v_upload_override != blank -%}, "uploadDate": {{ v_upload_override | json }}{%- endif -%}
}
</script>
          {%- assign v_printed = v_printed | plus: 1 -%}
        {%- endunless -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endfor -%}
  {%- endif -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  Breadcrumb: each ListItem is emitted inline (no join/split round-trip) so a
  title containing a "|" cannot corrupt positions or JSON. Every name/url is
  `| json`-escaped.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if want_breadcrumb and request.page_type == 'product' and product -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="breadcrumb">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": {{ shop.url | json }} },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ product.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: product.url | json }} }
  ]
}
</script>
{%- elsif want_breadcrumb and request.page_type == 'collection' and collection -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="breadcrumb">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": {{ shop.url | json }} },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ collection.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: collection.url | json }} }
  ]
}
</script>
{%- elsif want_breadcrumb and request.page_type == 'article' and article -%}
<script type="application/ld+json" data-contentpilot="breadcrumb">
{
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": {{ shop.url | json }} },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": {{ blog.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: blog.url | json }} },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": {{ article.title | json }}, "item": {{ shop.url | append: article.url | json }} }
  ]
}
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{%- comment -%}
  SEO tab Phase 3: 404 beacon. Only on 404 pages, reports the missing path +
  referrer to the app-proxy collector (Seo404Hit) so the merchant can see
  frequent 404s and one-click a redirect. Best-effort: sendBeacon with a fetch
  fallback, wrapped in try/catch so it can never disturb the storefront.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- if request.page_type == '404' -%}
<script>
  (function () {
    try {
      var payload = JSON.stringify({
        path: window.location.pathname + window.location.search,
        referrer: document.referrer || ''
      });
      var url = '/apps/contentpilot/seo-404';
      if (navigator.sendBeacon) {
        navigator.sendBeacon(url, new Blob([payload], { type: 'application/json' }));
      } else {
        fetch(url, { method: 'POST', headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' }, body: payload, keepalive: true });
      }
    } catch (e) { /* never break the storefront */ }
  })();
</script>
{%- endif -%}

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "JSON-LD Structured Data",
  "target": "body",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Adds schema.org JSON-LD (Product, Collection, Article, Organization, Breadcrumb, Video, FAQ) for Google rich results. Enable once - markup is generated automatically per page. Your theme may already emit some of these types itself; the app's Structured data section, step 3, measures which ones and tells you what is safe to switch on."
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_organization",
      "label": "Organization (all pages)",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_product",
      "label": "Product (product pages)",
      "info": "Most themes emit their own product markup. Both at once is not merely duplicated but INVALID: this block uses the same @id as the theme on purpose, because it is meant to replace it, so Google merges the two into one node and every field both sides deliver ends up twice. Check step 3 of the app's Structured data section before switching this on. Optional: set the product metafield \"custom.price_valid_until\" (type date) on a product whose price you actually guarantee until a fixed day, and it is published as priceValidUntil. Left unset, the property is omitted - the app never invents a price-guarantee date.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_collection",
      "label": "Collection (collection pages)",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_article",
      "label": "Article (blog post pages)",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_breadcrumb",
      "label": "Breadcrumb list",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_video",
      "label": "Video (product pages)",
      "info": "Emits VideoObject for videos in your product media AND for YouTube links that only live in a variant gallery, deduplicated per product. Google requires an upload date: for product media the app fills it in automatically during the sync, but a gallery link has no file behind it, so without the product metafield \"custom.video_upload_date\" (type date) that video gets no rich result - the property is omitted rather than guessed. The same metafield also overrides the automatic date. Vimeo gallery links are skipped: the rich result needs a thumbnail and none can be derived from a Vimeo link.",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "enable_faq",
      "label": "FAQ (product pages, from the \"custom.faq\" metafield)",
      "info": "Off by default - only enable once the product metafield \"custom.faq\" (a list of question/answer pairs) is populated.",
      "default": false
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Organization details"
    },
    {
      "type": "image_picker",
      "id": "logo",
      "label": "Logo (falls back to your theme brand logo)"
    },
    {
      "type": "textarea",
      "id": "social_urls",
      "label": "Social / profile URLs (one per line)",
      "info": "Added as Organization.sameAs (e.g. your Instagram, Facebook, X)."
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
